function isRawUserProfile(user: any): user is RawUserProfile {
  return (
    typeof user?.id === 'string' &&
    typeof user?.first_name === 'string' &&
    typeof user?.last_name === 'string' &&
    typeof user?.email_address === 'string' &&
    ['ACTIVE', 'SUSPENDED', 'PENDING'].includes(user?.status) &&
    typeof user?.last_login_at === 'string'
  );
}
```

This approach wins here because it eliminates the 'Partial-State' bug class, where a developer might add a new field to `TargetUserProfile` but forget to add the corresponding `typeof` check in the imperative block, leading to `undefined` being passed into the final object. By using a Type Guard (`isRawUserProfile`), the rest of the function operates on a guaranteed contract, making the mapping logic a pure transformation rather than a series of defensive checks.

